## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of 128-bit bit packing, non-overlapping fields, and in-place updates without loops. It exercises careful mask calculation and cross-boundary writes.

## Key Concepts
- Bit masks and clearing specific ranges
- Cross-boundary placement across lo/hi
- Safe in-place updates without branching
- Endianness-agnostic bit numbering

## Code Example
```c
void packHeader128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint8_t A, uint16_t B, uint16_t C, uint32_t D, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  uint64_t lo_mask = ((uint64_t)0xFF) | (((uint64_t)0xFFF) << 8) | (((uint64_t)0xFFFF) << 32);
  uint64_t hi_mask = ((uint64_t)0xFFFFFFFF) << 16;
  *out_lo = (lo & ~lo_mask) | (uint64_t)A | (((uint64_t)B) << 8) | (((uint64_t)C) << 32);
  *out_hi = (hi & ~hi_mask) | (((uint64_t)D) << 16);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if ranges could overlap and you needed deterministic behavior?
- How would you validate correctness across all inputs in a unit test?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses branch-free 128-bit rotation, a common primitive in crypto and low-level optimizations.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values via two 64-bit halves
- Branchless selection with masks
- Cross-half rotation logic and edge cases (n=0, n=64, n=127)

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, uint8_t n) {
  uint64_t n0 = n & 63;
  uint64_t m  = -((int)((n >> 6) & 1)); // 0 or -1
  uint64_t hi1 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo1 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t hi2 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo2 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = (hi1 & ~m) | (hi2 & m);
  r.lo = (lo1 & ~m) | (lo2 & m);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How might you test for constant-time properties across inputs?
- How would you extend to a constant-time generic rotate for arbitrary bit-widths?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement bit-twiddling without loops, using SWAR patterns for constant-time population count.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (Small/Word-level Parallelism)
- 64-bit masks and carry-free sums
- No intrinsics or branches; deterministic performance

## Code Example
```javascript
uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to count bits in 128-bit values? 
- What are the trade-offs vs. hardware popcnt instructions in modern CPUs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit arithmetic, two's-complement negation, and branchless masking, all crucial for low-level optimizations in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit representation via hi/lo limbs
- Two's-complement negation across limb boundaries
- Branchless bit masking using bitwise operations
- Edge cases: x==0 and bit in hi vs lo limb

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 extractLowestSetBit128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t loNeg = (~x.lo) + 1;
  uint64_t hiNeg = (~x.hi) + (loNeg == 0);
  uint128 mask;
  mask.hi = x.hi & hiNeg;
  mask.lo = x.lo & loNeg;
  return mask;
}
```

## Examples
- Input: `{hi: 0, lo: 0b1000}` → Output: `{hi: 0, lo: 0b1000}`
- Input: `{hi: 0b1000, lo: 0}` → Output: `{hi: 0b1000, lo: 0}`

